Wideband coherent point-to-multipoint (P2MP) interconnects based on wavelength division multiplexing and digital subcarrier multiplexing offer flexible capacity for geographically separated data centers. Their practical operation, however, is complicated by frequency-selective channel responses that produce strongly unequal SNR and generalized mutual information (GMI) across subcarriers and destination branches. Conventional transceivers compensate for this variation through CSI-driven bit loading, power loading, or entropy allocation. Such per-subcarrier adaptation increases control complexity and hardware specialization precisely when AI-scale inter-data center (DC) traffic requires predictable high-rate service. This paper presents Vandermonde-Orthogonal Matrix Division Multiplexing (VOMDM), a physical-layer multiplexing architecture that converts heterogeneous subcarrier responses into an equivalent flat-channel abstraction. By spreading each data stream over an orthogonal Vandermonde group, VOMDM avoids assigning a separate modulation and coding decision to every individual sub-carrier while preserving spectral efficiency. To remove the electronic bottleneck of the required matrix operations, we further co-design a microring-resonator (MRR) photonic processor that performs VOMDM transforms in the optical domain. Experiments on a 1.44-Tb/s comb-based coherent P2MP platform show that the proposed hardware-assisted architecture provides a low-complexity and high-capacity interconnect primitive for scalable AI data-center networking.